@ 17.7.2.1.4 - Midlands Middle English to Early Modern English
# citation: Pogostick Man, from FireSpeakerWiki contributors (2013), “English sound changes”. FireSpeakerWiki. <http://wiki.firespeaker.org/English_sound_changes> “[A] large number of cases that were ɑː have become ɔː subsequently for non-phonetic reasons, like laundry”
	ʊ > ɤ ! P_ and _l
	mb ŋɡ > m ŋ / _#
	tj sj dj zj > t͡ʃ ʃ d͡ʒ ʒ // _u:[+long] (perhaps only before stressed u:[+long]?)
	a ɑ {ɛ,ɪ,ɤ} > ɑ:[+long] ɔ:[+long] ɜ:[+long] / _ɹ{C,#}
	ɑʊ > ɑ:[+long] / _P
	ɑʊ > ɑ:[+long] / _N (sometimes)
	ɑʊ > ɔ:[+long] / else
	a > ɑ:[+long] / “in a few words, like ‘father’”
	a > æ / else
	əɪ əʊ > ɑi æʊ / “in some part͡s of South-Eastern England”
	əɪ əʊ > aɪ aʊ / “in most of Britain”
	e:[+long] o:[+long] > eɪ oʊ // _ɹ